\section{Define Run-Time Selection Against a Resolved Request}

Our diagram editor gives each drawable object a method
\texttt{paint(Surface)}. A \texttt{Circle} and a
\texttt{Label} override it. An instruction in a typed
interpreter should not ask ``find anything named paint''.
It asks for the virtual method resolved from a particular
owner and descriptor; the receiver selects an implementation
of that method. A \texttt{Label} can also declare
\texttt{paint(Preview)} without changing the meaning of the
original send. Return types, calling conventions, and
argument representation are already fixed by the resolved
request to the extent needed at the call boundary.

The JVM specification illustrates this split. A virtual
instruction refers to a symbolic constant-pool entry, which
is resolved, and a subsequent selection step uses the
receiver's class. Its specified behavior includes errors
and frame setup; a particular physical table layout is not
the semantic definition. \Cref{fig:visual-404} illustrates
two receivers for one resolved method.

