\section{Start With a Slow Path That Can Serve as an Oracle}

In a miniature single-inheritance runtime, build an
override map indexed by method-family identity, assigned
during class verification. Each class map contains its
locally declared overrides. A receiver without a local
implementation searches its parent. An abstract override
is an entry asserting that this class did not supply a
callable body; it is not a miss that permits the interpreter
to select a more distant implementation. A well typed
language may prevent abstract receivers entirely but the
metadata verifier should still establish the invariant.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Receiver & Local declaration & Result of base request \\
\hline
\texttt{Circle} & override & circle body \\
\texttt{Label} & override and overload & label override only \\
\texttt{BlankCircle} & none & inherited circle body \\
\texttt{null} & none & null-receiver error \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The descriptor still matters when the target has covariant
returns or another adaptation at the ABI boundary. A
runtime can install a bridge entry that returns the required
representation. A direct call to the body is valid only if
the caller performs the equivalent bridge or proves it
unnecessary for that use. Match on the method family, not
only the spelling and arity of the implementation.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Interpret the Send and Preserve the Calling Contract}

\begin{verbatim}
select(receiver_class, family):
    cls := receiver_class
    while cls != None:
        entry := cls.local_overrides.get(family)
        if entry is Abstract: raise no_concrete_method(family)
        if entry exists: return entry
        cls := cls.parent
    raise no_concrete_method(family)

send(frame, instruction):
    method := resolve_once(frame.constants, instruction.index)
    (receiver, args) := peek_operands(frame, method.descriptor)
    if receiver is null: raise null_receiver()
    entry := select(class_of(receiver), method.family)
    callee := make_frame(entry, return_to(frame, instruction))
    callee.locals[0] := receiver
    place_arguments(callee, args, method.descriptor)
    commit_call(frame, callee)
\end{verbatim}

This is a toy instruction, not a full JVM definition. In
particular it does not implement interface-default selection,
synchronized entry, or native methods. For this instruction,
resolution precedes the null check; if the symbolic reference
is invalid, the error should not be replaced by a null-receiver
error. The receiver and evaluated arguments remain in the
caller until the callee frame and its roots are installed.
If lookup or frame construction raises, unwinding observes
the language-defined state at the call.

\section{Lower to an Indirect Call Without Changing the Request}

A compiler can replace the superclass walk by loading a
class descriptor, reading a slot assigned to the method
family, and calling its address. A flattened table encodes
the result of the same selection for each class. The native
call still has the expected ABI, and it still checks null
unless the receiver is proven non-null or the runtime has
an equivalent trapping scheme. In a compiler IR, keep the
receiver and arguments explicit instead of hiding them all
inside descriptor manipulation.

\begin{verbatim}
ref := evaluate_receiver()
val := evaluate_surface()       // may log or raise
check_call_preconditions(ref)   // phase defined by language
cls := load_class(ref)
fn  := load_slot(cls, paint_family)
call_indirect desc_paint(fn, ref, val)
\end{verbatim}

If argument evaluation raises before the call, neither the
method lookup nor the callee's side effects may run ahead of
that exception in this toy order. Replaying evaluation on a
slow branch is also incorrect: an expression producing
\texttt{Surface} can update a metric. Move a guard across
effectful operations only with a proof that the observation
or exception order will be unchanged.

Test the interpreter and compiled path with identical event
traces rather than just checking the returned pixels. Include
a subclass inheriting an override, a same-spelled overload,
a null receiver with an effectful argument, and a symbolic
linkage failure. In a system permitting classes to load
after compilation, new classes must get complete descriptors
before publication. A compiled call with a layout-independent
fallback can keep using its receiver's method family even
when a new subclass appears; a call that guessed one target
needs an additional guard or validity proof.

\section{Compare Closed Class Dispatch With Open Message Lookup}

An untyped prototype interpreter may instead define
\texttt{receiver.paint} as a mutable property selection.
There is no resolved method family to index. A local
data property can shadow a function farther along the
prototype chain, and the first occurrence can therefore
produce a noncallable-property error. Do not keep walking
after finding it just because an earlier call found a
callable method. The cacheable fact is the result of this
language's property lookup and invocation protocol, which
is different from the typed slot selection above.

\begin{verbatim}
prototype_send(recv, name, args):
    owner := recv
    while owner exists:
        if owns_property(owner, name):
            value := read_property_as(owner, recv, name)
            require_callable(value)
            return invoke(value, recv, args)
        owner := prototype_of(owner)
    raise missing_property(name)
\end{verbatim}

The receiver passed to a method is still the original object,
not the prototype that owns the method. The example assumes
an acyclic chain, checked when prototype links are changed.
It also leaves accessors to \texttt{read\_property\_as};
if that operation invokes user code, a compiler cannot
replace it with a cached function unless it preserves those
effects or proves this particular property is a constant
data method. The slow path should be the baseline for
that proof. A test can install one routine on a prototype,
send through a child, then add a noncallable shadow to the
child and require the next send to fail. Also confirm
that the original send observed a field on the child, not
a same-spelled field on the prototype.

An implementation may give this operation and static-family
dispatch different IR opcodes. They have different effects
and failure modes: a property accessor may execute arbitrary
code before selection finishes, whereas a verified slot load
need not invoke a user hook. A compiler that represents both
as effect-free target selection cannot tell when motion across
an intervening store would change what the program observes.
